Hi [Manager],

I pulled the Illinois EPA Source Water Assessment Program factsheets for the =
eight water systems you listed (Farmer City, Springerton, Bartlett, Enfield, =
Crossville, Weldon, Norris City, Waynesville) and screened all 33 wells repor=
ted. The workbook is attached. Tab 1 lists every well with a Yes/No column fo=
r each criterion and a final "Meets ALL criteria?" column (filter on Yes); Ta=
b 2 holds only the qualifying wells.

Screening criteria: depth 160-200; aquifer description Sand & Gravel; well de=
scription contains none of abandoned, inactive, disconnected, emergency or se=
aled.

RESULT: 7 of 33 wells pass, in three systems.

Priority | Water system | Well ID / description | Depth | Reported pumpage | =
Comment
1 | Farmer City | WL47648 Well 9 | 188 | 26,478,500 | Highest reported pumpag=
e of the group; proven production
2 | Farmer City | WL47646 Well 7 | 180 | 21,976,200 | Second-highest pumpage =
of the group
3 | Weldon | WL45047 Well 3 | 167 | 5,500,000 | Best Weldon option; aquifer c=
ode 0909
4 | Weldon | WL45048 Well 4 | 163 | 3,503,040 | Passes; near the low end of t=
he depth window
5 | Waynesville | WL40006 Well 6 at the Village Hall | 162 | 4,319,500 | Pass=
es; near the low end of the depth window
6 | Farmer City | WL47647 Well 10 | 181 | 0 | Passes, but 0 pumpage reported;=
 confirm actual use
7 | Farmer City | WL01130 Well 11 | 192 | 0 | Passes, but 0 pumpage reported;=
 confirm actual use

Recommendation: Farmer City Wells 9 and 7 are the top options. They are the o=
nly qualifying wells with substantial reported pumpage, they sit mid-window o=
n depth (180-188), and four of the seven qualifying wells are in the Farmer C=
ity system. Weldon Wells 3 and 4 and Waynesville Well 6 are viable alternates=
. Farmer City Wells 10 and 11 qualify on paper, but the 0 pumpage entry needs=
 verification before we rely on them.

Systems with no qualifying wells: Bartlett (sand & gravel wells are 67 ft or =
99 ft, and Well 6 is also emergency; the rest are bedrock), Springerton (110 =
and 120 ft, abandoned), Enfield (all bedrock, 353-410 ft, abandoned), Crossvi=
lle (60-68 ft, abandoned), Norris City (sand & gravel and active, but only 97=
 and 124 ft).

Near misses: Waynesville Well 8 (207 ft, 7 ft over the limit); Weldon Well 6 =
(350 ft); Weldon Well 5 (293 ft, inactive).

Caveats and assumptions:

- All wells here are public water supply wells. Whether any system would sell=
 or share water with an industrial user, and in what volume, is not addressed=
 by this data. Pumpage is current use by the community, not available capacit=
y, so next steps are to contact the municipalities and check the permitting a=
nd water rights.

- The factsheet page does not label units. I assumed depth and setback are in=
 feet and pumpage is gallons per year. Please treat this as an assumption unt=
il confirmed.

- The Status column was reported as A/I. A few wells are marked A but carry a=
n excluded term (Bartlett Wells 4 and 6, "emergency"); I excluded these per y=
our criteria. Farmer City Well 4 is described as "transferred ownership" and =
is status I, and it is only 90 ft, so it fails regardless.

- Weldon Well 6 has no pumpage value reported; it fails on depth in any case.

- This is a desktop screen of state summary data only. It does not assess wat=
er quality, yield, treatment needs for electrolysis (deionization), or the lo=
ng-term sustainability of the aquifer.

Source: Illinois EPA, Source Water Assessment Program Factsheets, https://dat=
aservices.epa.illinois.gov/swap/factsheet.aspx (community water systems; sear=
ched by system name for IL0390150 Farmer City, IL1930450 Springerton, IL03141=
20 Bartlett, IL1930200 Enfield, IL1930150 Crossville, IL0390350 Weldon, IL193=
0350 Norris City, IL0390300 Waynesville). Retrieved 2026-11-05.

